# 5. System Design

This section details the abstract architectural design of the WatchWave platform. System design bridges the gap between the initial requirement specifications and the actual codebase implementation. The diagrams below visualize the data structures, operational boundaries, and dynamic flow of information across the Real-Time Streaming environment.

## 5.1 Entity-Relationship (ER) Model Diagram

The ER Diagram defines the logical structure of WatchWave's database (primarily mapped to Firebase Firestore NoSQL collections). It outlines the primary entities (Users, Rooms, Movies, Clips, Payments) and the relational mapping connecting them.

```mermaid
erDiagram
    USER ||--o{ WATCH_ROOM : "hosts"
    USER ||--o{ PAYMENT : "makes"
    USER ||--o{ CLIP : "creates"
    WATCH_ROOM ||--|| MOVIE : "currently streams"
    CLIP }o--|| MOVIE : "extracted from"

    USER {
        string uid PK "Firebase Auth ID"
        string name "Display Name"
        string email "Unique Email"
        string avatar "Base64 Compressed JPEG"
        string bio "Profile Description"
        boolean isOnline "Socket Presence Status"
        string activePlan "Free | Solo | PartyHub"
    }

    WATCH_ROOM {
        string roomId PK "Unique 6-char Code"
        string hostId FK "References User.uid"
        string currentMovieId FK "References Movie.movieId"
        boolean isPasswordProtected
        string hashedPassword "Optional Entry Lock"
        datetime createdAt
    }

    MOVIE {
        string movieId PK "TMDB ID / Internal ID"
        string title "Media Title"
        string description "Plot Synopsis"
        string videoUrl "CDN Endpoint URL"
        string posterUrl "TMDB Backdrop Image URL"
        string genre "Category Array"
        int matchPercentage "AI Computed Metric"
    }

    PAYMENT {
        string paymentId PK "Database Receipt ID"
        string userId FK "Subscriber ID"
        string razorpayOrderId "Gateway Reference"
        float amount "Transaction Value"
        string status "Success | Failed"
        datetime timestamp
    }

    CLIP {
        string clipId PK "Generated Hash"
        string userId FK "Creator ID"
        string sourceMovieId FK "Original Media ID"
        int startTime "Trim Start (Seconds)"
        int endTime "Trim End (Seconds)"
        string caption "Overlay Text"
    }
```

---

## 5.2 Data Flow Diagrams (DFD)

Data Flow Diagrams map how information propagates through the system boundaries, passing from external inputs through the core WatchWave logic controllers, and finally resting in database stores or client UIs.

### Level 0 Context DFD
The Context Diagram establishes the absolute system boundary of WatchWave, isolating it alongside the external human actors (Hosts/Viewers) and external 3rd-party APIs (Razorpay, TMDB, Google Gemini).

```mermaid
graph TD
    User([Standard Viewer])
    Host([Party Host])
    System(((WatchWave<br/>Streaming Engine)))
    Razorpay[[Razorpay API]]
    TMDB[[TMDB API]]
    Gemini[[Google Gemini AI]]

    User -- "Login Credentials, Room Join Requests, Real-Time Chat" --> System
    System -- "Video Stream Chunks, Sync Overlays, Subtitles" --> User
    
    Host -- "Create Room Cmd, Play/Pause/Seek Commands, Mute Users" --> System
    System -- "Host Dashboard UI, Participant Status Arrays" --> Host
    
    System -- "Order Payload" --> Razorpay
    Razorpay -- "Subscription Success Webhooks" --> System
    
    System -- "Content Queries (Movie ID)" --> TMDB
    TMDB -- "Posters, Ratings, Plot Metadata" --> System
    
    System -- "User Mood Description Prompts" --> Gemini
    Gemini -- "Filtered Media Recommendations Array" --> System

    classDef external fill:#2d3748,stroke:#4a5568,stroke-width:2px,color:#fff;
    classDef system fill:#d69e2e,stroke:#b7791f,stroke-width:4px,color:#000;
    class Razorpay,TMDB,Gemini external;
    class System system;
```

### Level 1 Operational DFD (Watch Party Flow)
This diagram drills down into the core application logic, specifically tracing the sophisticated bidirectional real-time data flows required to maintain a perfectly synchronized Watch Party.

```mermaid
graph TD
    Host([Room Host])
    Viewer([Remote Viewer])
    
    subgraph WatchWave Express Backend
        Auth(1.0 Authentication<br>Firebase Admin)
        RoomMgr(2.0 Room Management<br>In-Memory Store)
        SocketRouter(3.0 Sync Engine<br>Socket.IO Bus)
    end
    
    DB[(Firestore DB)]
    
    Host -- "1. Login Payload" --> Auth
    Auth -- "2. Verify JWT" --> DB
    Auth -- "3. Issue Bearer Token" --> Host
    
    Host -- "4. Generate Room (Code)" --> RoomMgr
    RoomMgr -- "5. Room Created" --> SocketRouter
    
    Viewer -- "6. Join Request (Room Code)" --> SocketRouter
    SocketRouter -- "7. Knock Event" --> Host
    Host -- "8. Accept Entry" --> SocketRouter
    SocketRouter -- "9. Grant Access" --> Viewer
    
    Host -- "10. Emit PLAY / PAUSE command" --> SocketRouter
    SocketRouter -- "11. Broadcast Synced Video State" --> Viewer
    
    Viewer -- "12. Emit Floating Emoji Reaction" --> SocketRouter
    SocketRouter -- "13. Render Emoji on All Screens" --> Host
    
    classDef process fill:#2b6cb0,stroke:#2c5282,color:#fff,stroke-width:2px;
    classDef db fill:#4a5568,stroke:#2d3748,color:#fff,shape:cylinder;
    class Auth,RoomMgr,SocketRouter process;
    class DB db;
```

### System Architecture Summary
1.  **ER Model:** Clearly delineates the core `WATCH_ROOM` relationship as the central orchestrator, demonstrating how `USER` interactions bind directly to isolated `MOVIE` playback sessions.
2.  **Level 0 Context:** Visually proves the platform's heavy reliance on external computational engines—offloading payment logic to Razorpay and generative matching to Gemini.
3.  **Level 1 Operation:** Visually maps the fundamental 13-step handshake required to achieve `<500ms` synchronized screening stability utilizing intermediate authentication and the central Node.js Socket bus.
